 [^preface-room-history]: In 1968, Ivan E. Sutherland's experimental head-mounted display put its viewer inside a room drawn in wireframe. Turning the head changed the view; walls, ceiling and floor were already part of the experiment. See Ivan E. Sutherland, [“A Head-Mounted Three Dimensional Display”](https://doi.org/10.1145/1476589.1476686) (1968), especially “Results”; [accessible paper](https://archive.aec.at/media/assets/636b23b07bfd1b4f1d1154827ac9d9ab.pdf). The wireframe room and response to head movement are described there. The acknowledgments name a collective effort, and the funding note names ARPA, the Office of Naval Research and Bell Telephone Laboratories. This is one historical encounter with these problems, not a claim that the museum follows a single line of descent.
 
 [^preface-other-uses]: The [DIVE IN manual](https://www.pina.si/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/DIVEin_Manual_EN.pdf), §2.3.1, includes household appliances among equipment for DIY microbiology. Izadi and colleagues' [KinectFusion](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/kinectfusion-real-time-3d-reconstruction-and-interaction-using-a-moving-depth-camera/) (2011) reconstructs surfaces from a moving Kinect depth camera. These examples concern equipment entering another practice; the laboratory remains an arrangement of tools, materials and knowledge.
+
+[^preface-folding-future]: Jacques Derrida, *Specters of Marx* (French 1993; English translation by Peggy Kamuf, 1994), “Exordium,” relates responsibility to those no longer present and those not yet here; see the [publisher's preview](https://api.pageplace.de/preview/DT0400.9781040559963_A63760427/preview-9781040559963_A63760427.pdf), pp. xxiii–xxiv, and [“Spectres of Marx,” *New Left Review* I/205 (1994)](https://newleftreview.org/issues/i205/articles/jacques-derrida-spectres-of-marx). *Anticipatory hauntology* describes Ada's emphasis here; it is not presented as Derrida's own term or a prediction that teachers or students will disappear. A related educational research-creation approach appears in Jo Albin-Clark's [“Data-ghost dialogues”](https://research.edgehill.ac.uk/en/publications/data-ghost-dialogues-playing-and-questioning-future-directions-wi/) (2024). The available conference abstract describes 3D digital artworks and imagined dialogues that question absence, ethical responsibility and future research directions. Ada's ghost student and ghost teacher are our interpretation of this project's changing pedagogical relationship, not the same apparatus as Albin-Clark's data-ghosts.
